  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{x+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for all values of $x\\in [-2,0].$  So there is no solution for ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\nSo, the only critical points are the endpoints $x=-2,0$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-2+3)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)=0 \\\\ f(0) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+3)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)≈-1.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-2)=0,$ so the global maximum is $0.$\n\n- The least value is $f(0)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3),$ so the global minimum is $-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14150956/assets/8578b190927c38e37fc5956d1b953539e41ea3e25f2e6ced48f21086722fdd43.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
